\title{Smart Contract Specification}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-05}
\meta{toc}{true}

\p{
    [Smart contracts](dhsorens-000Y) can have [deceptively difficult specifications](dhsorens-000L).
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001N} % dexter2 verification

\p{
    The problem that Dexter2 illustrates is that smart contracts have social, game theoretic, and
    crypto-economic properties that are difficult to state in a prose specification, much less in a
    formal way. This in contrast with something like [ZK circuits, which are an interesting, contrasting case](dhsorens-0052): a hash function's specification
    is an equation, so fixing an interface in Lean is enough to make optimisation safe — a discipline that would have bought nothing here.
    % The best contract specifications of smart contracts use time-tested approaches to capture the most important aspects of code security—\em{e.g.} solvency, sovereignty, or key safety properties.
    % If there is a liquidity pool of some kind involved, then a formal specification might target solvency properties, or properties of sovereignty over one's assets.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001P} % vulnerabiliteis due to poor specs